    "main_topic": [
      {
        "h_title": "Kaeng Khiao Wan: Thailand’s Iconic Green Curry",
        "main_title": "Kaeng Khiao Wan: Thailand’s Iconic Green Curry",
        "author": "Thailand Foundation",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 1,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Table of Contents",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Thai cuisine is renowned worldwide for its bold flavors, aromatic spices, and diverse dishes. From the famous Pad Thai to Tom Yum Goong, Thai food has captivated people across the globe. Among these beloved dishes, one that stands out for its unique taste and vibrant appearance is Kaeng Khiao Wan, or Thai Green Curry.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": [
          {
            "text": "What is Kaeng Khiao Wan?",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "What is Kaeng Khiao Wan?",
        "main_title": "Kaeng Khiao Wan: Thailand’s Iconic Green Curry",
        "author": "Thailand Foundation",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "The name Kaeng Khiao Wan (แกงเขียวหวาน) translates to “sweet green curry” in Thai. However, the word “sweet” ( wan – หวาน) does not refer to the taste but rather to the creamy green color of the curry. Kaeng Khiao Wan is a rich and aromatic Thai curry made with coconut milk, green curry paste, and a choice of protein, such as chicken, beef, or fish balls. It is a perfect balance of spicy, savory, and slightly sweet flavors. Served with steamed jasmine rice or Khanom Chin, this dish is a staple of Thai cuisine and a favorite among locals and international food lovers alike.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "History of Kaeng Khiao Wan",
        "main_title": "Kaeng Khiao Wan: Thailand’s Iconic Green Curry",
        "author": "Thailand Foundation",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Thai curries were originally clear and broth-based. Over time, the introduction of coconut milk and curry pastes transformed Thai cuisine, leading to the creation of richer and creamier dishes. Kaeng Khiao Wan is believed to have emerged during the early 20th century, around the reign of King Rama VI to Rama VII (1910–1935). Traditionally, the green curry paste itself is an adaptation of the red curry paste, replacing dried red chilies with fresh green chilies to achieve its signature color.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Today, Kaeng Khiao Wan is one of Thailand’s most internationally recognized dishes. It is commonly served in Thai households, street food stalls, and high-end restaurants. The dish has also gained global fame, appearing on menus worldwide and being featured in international food rankings. With its bold flavors and creamy texture, Thai Green Curry continues to captivate food lovers everywhere, cementing its place as a staple of Thai cuisine.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "800 ml coconut milk",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "70 g green curry paste",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "100 g Thai eggplant, cut into pieces",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "50 g pea eggplant",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "3 kaffir lime leaves",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "2 red and green chili peppers",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "15 g Thai basil leaves",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "1 tbsp fish sauce",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "1 tbsp palm sugar",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Your choice of meat",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "Instructions",
        "main_title": "Kaeng Khiao Wan: Thailand’s Iconic Green Curry",
        "author": "Thailand Foundation",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 3,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Sauté the Curry Paste – Heat 200 ml coconut milk in a pan, add green curry paste, and stir until fragrant.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Cook the meat – Add bite-sized meat pieces and stir until cooked. Season with fish sauce and palm sugar. Pour in the remaining coconut milk and bring to a boil.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Add Vegetables & Serve – Add kaffir lime leaves, Thai eggplant, and pea eggplant. Simmer until tender, then stir in chili peppers and basil. Serve hot.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Values Behind the Dish",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Beyond its delicious taste, Kaeng Khiao Wan embodies the deep-rooted culinary heritage of Thailand. This dish reflects the Thai philosophy of using fresh, local ingredients, showcasing a strong respect for nature and sustainability. Key components such as fresh green chilies, Thai basil, kaffir lime leaves, and coconut milk are sourced from Thailand’s abundant natural resources, emphasizing the connection between food and the environment.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
